  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA ridge of high pressure will keep the weather quiet and comfortable \ntoday and much of Saturday.\n\nA low pressure system will approach from the Midwest Saturday, \nbringing showers and storms along with it for the weekend. The \nhighest chance of rain will be on Sunday as the center of low \npressure tracks across the middle Ohio Valley. Models suggest the \npotential of 500-1,000 J/kg of MLCAPE developing by Sunday afternoon \nin the warm sector ahead of a cold front with 30-40 kts of deep \nlayer shear. A few strong thunderstorms may develop Sunday afternoon \nif enough destabilization occurs, and the main threat would be \nlocalized damaging wind gusts. Precipitable water values will range \nfrom 1.80 to 2.20 inches during this timeframe, so these \nthunderstorms will contain heavy downpours. If training \nthunderstorms develop, localized flooding could be a concern.\n\nShowers and thunderstorms will remain possible Monday and Tuesday as \nthe upper-level low remains parked over the eastern U.S. Models \nproject the upper-level low escaping northeastward into New England \nWednesday and Thursday, but showers and thunderstorms will remain \npossible across our region as the mid-level flow turns \nsouthwesterly. This will bring a stream of mid- level vorticity\ninto the middle Ohio Valley from the South.",
